Local patch 0009 (qwen3.8-flash-next-exl3-tensorfold): decode CUDA graphs captured once per slot size and kept per slot.

Why: decode "slowed with context" (52 tok/s at 2k, ~34 at 8.8k and 33k), but warm rounds cost the same at every length
(~40 ms of GPU work). The slow rounds were CUDA graph captures (~20 window shapes x ~130 ms) repeated: (1) graphs were
keyed by power-of-two context buckets from 8192, so each conversation crossing 16k/32k/64k... captured again; (2) when a
lone stream moved into the graph slot while that slot held another conversation's kept prompt (common with several
conversations), the slot was re-pointed and its graphs dropped; (3) growing the slot dropped them too.
What:
- qwen4_exp/cuda/graphs.py _bucket: the slot's capacity (one graph per window shape and buffer parity). Measured: decode
  at 0.3k/8.8k/33k tokens identical to the bucketed graphs, also with the slot grown to 131k.
- qwen4_exp/cuda/multi_solo.py: _point_solo keeps the previous graph slot's graphs (slot, capacity) and gives a slot its
  own back when it is the graph slot again with unchanged buffers; _state_changed drops a slot's kept graphs before any
  reallocation (and _relocate_kept now calls it before resizing its spare). Graph captures allocate no memory here
  (static buffers): measured 0 MiB per graph set. The two-rank plan replay (multi_plan) is unchanged.
Measured (three fixed conversations of 0.3k/8.8k/33k tokens in rotation, greedy): second round 36/35/46 -> 45/46/45
tok/s; replies identical (token hashes) to before. Growth of the slot still captures once (once per size a server
reaches).
Tests: tests/test_flash_capacity_dispatch.py's bucket test now asserts one capture across contexts (the old behaviour
was the bug); tests/test_flashnext_round_plan.py adds test_graph_slot_switch_keeps_each_slots_graphs.

